- [ ] **(Eng)** Runbooks: provider outage, stuck escrow, webhook never arrived, wrong payout, kill switch, mailbox blocked, LLM gateway down.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Disaster recovery: region outage plan, RPO / RTO targets.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Cost dashboards: LLM, Apify (daily run budget exists), Tavily, Atlas, hosting.
- [ ] **(Eng)** The standalone API gateway (`services/api_gateway`) still has TODOs for signature verification, idempotency keys and request enrichment — finish them or do not deploy it.

### 8 Security and access
- [ ] **(Eng)** Token revocation delay is the introspection cache (60 s, `AUTH_INTROSPECTION_CACHE_SECONDS`) — confirm acceptable, shorten for money approvers.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Encrypt sensitive fields at rest (bank details, KYC documents, phone numbers).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Prompt-injection defence for inbound e-mails: the drafting agents already treat counterparty text as data — add tests with hostile e-mails.

### 9 Operator console (portless-ui)
- [ ] **(Eng)** Show the supplier's facility address and whether the inspection waits for it on the deal page, with an edit field (backend `PATCH /deals/{id}/parties/supplier` books the waiting inspection).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Show superseded match gates as "closed — another pair was confirmed", not as rejected.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Show prices to the operator in USD with the supplier's INR figure beside it where relevant.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Readiness page: show `GET /health/readiness` so operators see what is stubbed.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Money screens: clear four-eyes state (who signed, who is still needed), tranche order, payout cap and retention.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Error and empty states for every page; SSE reconnect banner.

### 10 Data quality
- [ ] **(Ops)** Real product catalogue and corridors (1.5).
- [ ] **(Ops)** Directory hygiene: duplicate companies, wrong countries, bounced e-mails (`email-list-qualifier-ai` result per address).
- [ ] **(Ops)** Price book cold start: seed observed prices per HS code and market so the match board's estimates are not empty for the first deals.
- [ ] **(Ops)** Product tree ids, packing standards, AQL and quality specs per product line (QIMA booking needs them).

### 11.1 Capture money already owed to the exporter — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Export incentives as income**: RoDTEP credits and duty drawback where the product qualifies — put them on the cost sheet as income per deal, track the claim and the credit received. Confirm eligibility for a merchant exporter per HS code with the CA.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **GST working capital**: export under LUT (no IGST outflow) and procure at the merchant-exporter concessional rate where eligible (3.3) — less cash locked per deal.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **FX margin**: buy in INR, sell in USD; time the conversion, compare the AD bank rate with the escrow provider's rate, and add a small FX buffer to supplier offers. Optional forward cover for large deals.

### 11.2 Cut costs on every shipment — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Freight quotes from more than one source** (Maersk + forwarders) and book the cheapest that meets the date; today Maersk is booked by default.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Container fill**: maximise MT per container (20 ft vs 40 ft, bag weight) in the cost sheet and the offer quantity.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Inspection cost**: compare inspector quotes per booking; offer the buyer a cheaper inspection plan when it is enough.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Escrow provider by cost**: pick the cheaper provider per deal size when the buyer has no preference; pass the escrow fee to the buyer as a line item where allowed.

### 11.3 Price better — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Margin by corridor and buyer**: learn from accepted / countered indicative offers (outcomes already recorded) to set the target margin per HS code × market × buyer tier instead of one floor.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Value-added services priced explicitly**: private label packing, fumigation, palletising, extra inspections — a markup per service, shown in the offer.

### 11.4 Win more and repeat — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Repeat orders and annual contracts**: a "reorder" button, reminders before the buyer's next usual order date, framework contracts with monthly lifts.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Buyer scoring for outreach**: focus the funnel on buyers most likely to close (reply rate, import history, fit score) — less outreach cost per deal.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Funnel analytics: scans → prospects → interested → matched → PI → paid, with time and cost per step.

### 12 Buyer and supplier validation (Opportunity Radar)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Proof that a buyer actually imports this product.** Today a verified buyer is a real company with a working mailbox and a clean sanctions result, not a proven importer of the HS code. Needs import records (bill-of-lading data): paid Volza / ImportGenius, an Apify plan that can run the Store importer actors, or a new PortLess actor on public US customs bill-of-lading data (US-only coverage). See the decision below.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Outreach and RFQ only to verified counterparties (switch, e.g. `OUTREACH_VERIFIED_ONLY` / `RFQ_VERIFIED_ONLY`): today a failed buyer / supplier is never contacted, but an unverified one still is. Decide first (decisions).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Sanctions "review" queue: counterparties whose screening returned `review` (possible match or a list unavailable) listed for staff, cleared or failed with a reason, audited like the compliance hold clearances; today they simply stay "unverified".
- [ ] **(Eng)** Re-check schedule: sanctions lists change — re-screen verified buyers / suppliers that are linked to open prospects or deals when their result passes `TRADE_MIN_VALIDATION_MAX_AGE_DAYS`, not only when a new scan finds them again.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Confidence score counts the check: today `confidence_score` ignores validation (a corridor with 0 verified counterparties can score the same as one with 5). Add points for verified buyers / suppliers and show it in the radar's Confidence tooltip.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Pair matching in the "All" view: mark or down-rank a pair with an unverified side; the Best Match column in "All" can currently name an unverified pair.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Foreign buyer company existence beyond the website: LEI (GLEIF, already returned by the screening actor), EU VIES for EU buyers (returned), and a company-register / domain-age check for other countries; show them on the badge.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Console: show the supplier's `MatchResult.verification` + reasons on the prospect / deal page, and `BuyerCandidate.validation_status` in the outreach funnel list (backend fields exist, the deal pages do not show them yet).
- [ ] **(Ops)** Directory hygiene with the check: run "Check now" on worldwide-search companies before they are used (they are never checked automatically — cost), and a bulk "check all unverified" action with a cost estimate first.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation: radar verified view hides unverified / failed, failed buyer never gets outreach, failed supplier never becomes the supplier party, reuse of a fresh result (no second actor run), no Apify token → nothing verified; browser case for the "Verified only / All" toggle and the directory filter.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Cost: show the validation actor runs per scan in the digest / cost dashboard (`APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY` budget is shared with discovery — a large scan can starve the check).

### 13.1 Keep a qualifying opportunity open — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** Switch `DEAL_COMPOSITION_ENABLED` (off by default until 13.4 is done): a prospect whose best pair scores at or above the threshold (`COMPOSITION_MIN_SCORE`, default = `MATCH_MIN_PROBABILITY`) is never dropped while one qualifying buyer and one qualifying supplier are still in play. A buyer declining, a supplier declining or one funnel running out no longer ends it; `deal_watcher` does not count a prospect with live offers as a stall.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Stop fixing the pair before negotiation. Today `matchmaking.confirm_match` sets one buyer + one supplier and `_withdraw_other_proposals` closes every other pair; the outreach funnel turns the first interested buyer into the buyer party (`outreach.py`, status `selected`) and stops contacting others; the RFQ winner becomes the supplier party. With composition on, every interested verified buyer and every quoting verified supplier stays a **candidate in negotiation**; buyer / supplier parties are set only by the composition decision (13.3).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Outreach keeps going after the first "interested" reply, up to `COMPOSITION_MAX_BUYERS` per prospect, so there is more than one buyer to compose for.
- [ ] **(Eng)** RFQ asks more than the top 3 when the buyers' total quantity is above the shortlisted suppliers' stated capacity (`RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS` is the cap).

### 13.2 Negotiate with every candidate on its own terms — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** One negotiation thread per counterparty. Today a `NegotiationSession` holds exactly one buyer and one supplier (`agents/negotiation_agent/schemas.py`). Each buyer thread negotiates the buyer's full requirement (`BuyerRequirement`: grade, quantity, incoterm, destination port, target price, delivery window, packing, inspection plan, escrow provider). Each supplier thread negotiates the supplier's full ability (`SupplierOffer`: grade, FOB / EXW price, MOQ, monthly capacity, port of loading, goods-ready date, packing, facility address).
- [ ] **(Eng)** A negotiation ends at a **firm offer**, not an agreement. Buyer: "Q MT of grade G at P, CIF port X, shipment by D, valid until V". Supplier: "up to Q MT of grade G at P FOB port Y, ready by D, valid until V". Store each one as a `FirmOffer` record with its validity. The e-mails say "subject to final order confirmation". Until composition there is no `CONFIRM_NEGOTIATION_AGREEMENT`, no PI and no firm order request.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Pricing per thread (`pricing.py`): the buyer band comes from the best supply mix currently available and the supplier ceiling from the best buyer price currently available. Today each comes from the one counterpart of the session. The margin floor is checked on the combination (13.3) as well as on each leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Partial quantity is not a reason to drop a supplier. When a supplier's capacity or offer is below what the buyers need, ask it for its maximum quantity and ready date, keep it, and ask the other suppliers for the rest of the quantity at the same grade.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Ask for a firm offer's validity and refresh it before it expires (one reminder, then mark it `expired`). An expired offer never enters a composition without a fresh price.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Privacy holds with N parties per prospect: a buyer never learns how many suppliers there are or who they are, and a supplier never learns about the buyers or the other suppliers. Extend `email_safety.preflight`, the draft leak checks and `knowledge_filter` from "the other party" to "every other party of the prospect".

### 13.3 Deal composition agent — choose the best deal after negotiation — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** New `shared/orchestration/composition.py`. Code makes the decision; the LLM only explains it, and no money decision depends on the LLM. Input: every live `FirmOffer` of the prospect, plus supplier offers of other prospects with the same HS code that are still valid. Output: proposed deals of one buyer × 1..N suppliers. Example: the buyer needs 1000 MT, supplier A offers 800 MT and supplier B 200 MT, so one deal is made with one buyer and two supplier legs. The objective is the highest expected margin after landed cost (each supplier's inland transport, port, freight plan from 13.5, one inspection per facility, escrow fee).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Hard rules for every combination:
  - compatible grade / spec;
  - every supplier's MOQ respected;
  - total within `COMPOSITION_QTY_TOLERANCE_PCT` of the buyer's quantity;
  - every leg ready before the buyer's shipment date;
  - ports of loading the same or consolidatable (13.5);
  - every party verified and clear of compliance holds, including related-party checks between the suppliers;
  - at most `COMPOSITION_MAX_SUPPLIERS` legs (start at 2–3);
  - margin floor on the total and on each leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** No decision the moment a negotiation ends. Re-run the composition when an offer arrives, changes, is withdrawn or nears expiry, and when the buyer's deadline gets close. The agent answers **close now** (before the earliest validity in the best combination expires) or **wait** (more quotes pending that could beat it, with the expected gain). Always show the best single-supplier deal next to the composed one.
- [ ] **(Eng)** New gate `CONFIRM_DEAL_COMPOSITION` (L0, money-related: never auto-approved by a value threshold) showing the combination, the per-leg quantity / price / port / ready date, the margin per leg and in total, the freight plan, the risks and the runner-up combinations. APPROVE sets the buyer party and the supplier legs, sends each supplier a firm order request (`supplier_commitment` per leg), then confirms the buyer's agreement and issues the PI at the combined quantity.
- [ ] **(Eng)** A leg that declines or stays silent after APPROVE triggers a new composition from the remaining offers for that leg's quantity only. This generalises today's single standby supplier. If no valid combination is left, raise `deal.exception` `COMPOSITION:no_cover`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Candidates that were not chosen stay in the pool with their offers for the next composition or a second deal. They get no "closed" e-mail while their offer is valid. `outcomes.py` records the predicted vs realised margin of every composition for calibration.
- [ ] **(Eng)** `GET /deals/{id}/composition` (live offers, candidate combinations, score breakdown, why one won, what would change the answer) and `POST /deals/{id}/composition/run` (staff).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Console (portless-ui): a composition card on the prospect page with offers per side, the proposed combination, alternatives and validity countdowns. The approval card shows the legs.

### 13.4 Running a deal with more than one supplier — Deal composition after negotiation
Everything after the match assumes one supplier party today: PO, QC, loading, payouts, retention, ledger, documents, compliance, notifications.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Data model: `DealRecord.supplier_legs` (supplier, quantity, price, basis, port of loading, ready date, facility address, PO, QC orders, status). Money, QC and documents read the legs instead of the single supplier party. A migration turns every existing deal into one leg; one-supplier deals behave exactly as today.
- [ ] **(Eng)** One PO per leg, with the margin guard on each leg and on the total. PO acknowledgement, goods-ready date and facility address are tracked per leg, and the watcher sends reminders per leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** QC per facility: one QIMA order per leg, loading supervision and seals per leg. The deal's `qc_status` passes only when every leg passed. A failed leg is replaced (13.3 recomposition) or that leg's quantity is cancelled with the buyer's consent; one leg does not take the whole deal into `DISPUTE`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Money: the buyer still has one escrow. Payouts, supplier retention, `available_for_payout`, the ledger and the journal are kept per leg (released tranches split pro rata by leg value). The deal is `PAID` only when every leg is paid in full. Four-eyes and the compliance money block apply per payout.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Documents. The buyer side is unchanged: one PI / Sales Contract / CI / PL at the combined quantity, and no supplier is named. The CI / PL list the containers and seals of every leg. On the supplier side, each PO and notice names only that supplier's own leg. Whether the SDF / Shipping Bill is one or one per port of loading follows the CHA decision.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Compliance: trade check, screening and related-party check for every leg. The shipment check applies the quantity tolerance to the total loaded against the PI and to each leg against its PO.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Deal board / timeline / evidence pack: a status per leg plus the aggregate (e.g. "QC 1 of 2 passed"); the evidence pack has a section per leg.

### 13.5 Freight — fewer containers and cheaper boxes — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** Container plan instead of a fixed 25 MT per box. Today both `trade_costs.py` (`round(qty / 25 + 0.49)`) and `MaerskConnector.build_booking` (`MAERSK_MT_PER_CONTAINER`, 25) assume 25 MT. Plan per product line: payload and volume limits by equipment (20 ft / 40 ft / 40 HC), bag size and density from the packing spec, and the remainder as a smaller box or LCL. Suggest rounding the offer quantity to full containers when that raises the margin. The cost sheet shows the plan.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Inside a multi-supplier deal, compare per leg and pick the cheapest option that meets the ready dates:
  - (a) each supplier stuffs its own containers at its own port;
  - (b) truck one leg to the other leg's port or a CFS and stuff together;
  - (c) send a small leg as LCL.

  The inspector supervises each stuffing point. The choice feeds the composition score (13.3).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Get freight quotes from the endpoints that exist: `CogoportConnector.quote` (already FCL / LCL; pass container type, cargo weight and LCL volume), `FreightosConnector.quote` and Maersk. `MaerskConnector.quote` today returns only the contract rate from `MAERSK_RATE_USD_PER_CONTAINER`; confirm the Maersk Product Offers / spot-rate API on the account and wire it. Compare quotes for each container plan and record the chosen quote with its source on the cost sheet. This overlaps 11.2 "freight quotes from more than one source".

### 13.6 Apify actors for quality trade and trade information — Deal composition after negotiation
Build each one with `/actor-pipeline` on the `rich_minds` account. That plan cannot run Store actors, so they must be PortLess's own actors. Each one needs a connector + normaliser in `shared/connectors/apify.py`, a readiness check, a place in the `APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY` budget, and pinned stub mode in tests.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Supplier quality / certification check**: FSSAI licence, APEDA RCMC, Spices Board / commodity-board registration, organic (NPOP), and ISO / BRC / HACCP certificates from public registries and the supplier's site. Feeds supplier trust, the composition's grade fit, and the documents the buyer asks for.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Rejection history**: EU RASFF notifications and US FDA import refusals by product and by Indian exporter. A risk flag per supplier and per HS × market; a recent refusal forces the composition gate to show it.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation:
  - 1000 MT buyer covered by 800 + 200 MT suppliers, through to PAID;
  - supplier B declines after APPROVE, the composition re-runs for 200 MT;
  - an expired offer is excluded;
  - margin floor broken on the total although one leg clears it;
  - a buyer e-mail never reveals the number of suppliers;
  - one leg fails QC, only that leg is replaced;
  - payouts per leg and PAID only when both legs are paid.

  Plus an LLM journey UC-17: like UC-15, with one buyer and two suppliers.

### 14.1 Legal entity — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal)** After incorporation: first board meeting, statutory auditor appointed within 30 days (ADT-1), commencement of business declaration (INC-20A) within 180 days, share certificates issued.
- [ ] **(Biz/Legal)** Founders' / shareholders' agreement (roles, equity, vesting, who signs and who approves money).
- [ ] **(Legal)** Trademark application for "PortLess" (name and logo) in the relevant classes (trading / logistics services).

### 14.2 Tax and trade registrations — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Udyam (MSME) registration — free; gives priority-sector lending, protection against late payment, and access to MSME export schemes.
- [ ] **(Legal)** FSSAI licence for food products — confirm with the CA / FSSAI which licence a merchant exporter of your products needs (central licence is typical for exporters).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Plant-quarantine registration for agri exports so the phytosanitary certificate can be applied for (also 3.5).
- [ ] **(Legal)** State registrations: Shops & Establishments licence for the office, professional tax (employer and directors).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Portals the business must use: DGFT, ICEGATE, GST, e-way bill (inland truck to port, also 3.5) and e-invoicing once turnover crosses the threshold.

### 14.3 Bank and payment accounts — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Pre-shipment / packing credit line with the bank (finances the supplier advance when a buyer's escrow is not yet released) and post-shipment credit if needed.
- [ ] **(Biz)** ECGC export credit insurance (policy and buyer limits) — cover against buyer default or country risk outside the escrow.
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Open marine cargo insurance policy with an insurer or broker (also 1.4 `PORTLESS_MARINE_POLICY`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Accounting software (Tally / Zoho Books) from day one, chart of accounts agreed with the CA (also 4).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Forward-contract (FX hedging) facility with the bank for large USD receipts (also 11.1).

### 14.4 Brand, website and contact details — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Business phone and WhatsApp Business number (local suppliers often answer only there), shown on the website and in the signature (`PORTLESS_PHONE`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** LinkedIn company page and a Google Business Profile for the office — buyers check both before replying to a first e-mail.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company profile / brochure PDF, letterhead, rubber stamp for signed export documents, visiting cards.
- [ ] **(Biz)** D-U-N-S number (Dun & Bradstreet, free) — many foreign buyers and their banks look a supplier up by it.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Fill the company profile settings once these exist: `PORTLESS_LEGAL_NAME`, `PORTLESS_WEBSITE`, `PORTLESS_CONTACT_EMAIL`, `PORTLESS_PHONE`, `PORTLESS_IEC`, `PORTLESS_GSTIN` (also 1.4).

### 14.5 Advisers and service providers — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company secretary (ROC filings) and a trade lawyer for the contracts (also 3.6).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Accounts with the providers the workflow uses: QIMA (inspection), Maersk (carrier) and one freight forwarder as a back-up (also 1.2, 2.3).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Courier account (DHL / FedEx / Aramex) for sending original documents (B/L, certificate of origin) to buyers' banks and samples to buyers.

### 14.6 Office, people and records — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Name board at the registered office (GST verification may check it) and a filing system for original documents (export documents must be kept for years, also 3.7).
- [ ] **(Biz)** People for the roles the platform expects: trade desk operator, money approver (second person for four-eyes), compliance officer (also 4, 8).

### 14.7 Product and market requirements — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Per destination: what the buyer's country needs before the goods arrive — e.g. US FDA food-facility registration and prior notice, Gulf halal certificate / SFDA (Saudi), EU pesticide residue limits (MRL) and certificate of analysis. Record it per product line in the catalogue.

### 14.8 Ongoing compliance calendar — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** One calendar with an owner for every recurring filing: monthly GST returns (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B), TDS returns, IEC annual update (April – June), LUT renewal (April), RCMC renewal, ROC annual filings (AOC-4, MGT-7), directors' KYC (DIR-3 KYC), income-tax return and tax audit, DSC renewal, insurance renewals.

