## Appendix — background per area

### 1 Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)

Each item is a failing or warning readiness check today.

### 2 Provider contracts not yet confirmed

Each provider was built from public documentation; these points were not confirmed by the provider and must be checked against a real account before money moves.

### 3 Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)

PortLess is the exporter of record, buys from Indian suppliers and sells to foreign buyers. Every point needs a lawyer / chartered accountant's sign-off; the seed lists in the code are not the law.

### 5.5 Add suppliers manually (local suppliers)

The trade desk knows local suppliers that no scan will find. Today they cannot be added so that
the workflow takes over:
- the supplier directory (`trade_counterparties`) is written only by a scan — there is no add / edit endpoint and no console screen (buyers can be added to a deal, suppliers cannot);
- a deal created by hand with a supplier party but no scan opportunity gets **no RFQ**: the RFQ leg finds no candidates (`_rfq_candidates` reads only Match Engine results or the opportunity) and leaves the deal parked in MATCHING;
- `POST /deals/{id}/supplier-offers` (an offer entered by staff) runs neither the GST / IEC verification nor the match board — someone has to call `/match/evaluate` by hand.
Backend
Console (portless-ui)
QA

### 11 New features to raise profit per deal (product)

Ordered by expected impact on margin. Each should be measured on closed deals (realised margin vs cost sheet).

### 12 Buyer and supplier validation (Opportunity Radar)

**Where the check belongs: the radar first, the deal again.** A company that is sanctioned, fake or
unreachable should never be shown as an opportunity's buyer or supplier, never get an outreach
e-mail and never become a deal party, so the first check runs in the scan, before the opportunity
is saved, on the companies the radar would show (top N per role, results reused for 30 days to keep
Apify cost down). The deal keeps its own checks because facts change between the scan and the
money: the buyer is screened again before the PI, the supplier's GST / IEC are verified at quote
intake, and trade compliance runs at deal creation and before the PI. Checking only on the deal
board (the old design) meant the radar listed unchecked companies and the outreach funnel could
pitch one before anyone looked.

Verdict rules: `verified` only on positive answers (sanctions clear + deliverable or catch-all
e-mail + no registry red flag); `failed` only on negative answers (sanctions block, GST / IEC
inactive, DGFT denied); everything else `unverified`. Without an Apify token nothing is verified.

### 13 Deal composition after negotiation (more buyers, more suppliers, cheaper freight)

**The idea.** A scan gives many buyer and supplier candidates per opportunity. Today PortLess picks
one buyer and one supplier *before* negotiating: the match board's CONFIRM_MATCH or the first
interested buyer plus the RFQ winner. After that, every other candidate is closed, and the deal
fails if that one pair fails. The new flow keeps a good opportunity open. It negotiates with every
qualifying buyer on the buyer's full requirement and with every qualifying supplier on the
supplier's full ability, collects firm offers with a validity date, and only then lets a
composition agent decide the most profitable deal from what was actually offered.

**Example.** A buyer needs 1000 MT. Supplier A can supply 800 MT, and supplier B offers 200 MT of
the same grade. The deal becomes one buyer with two supplier legs, not a lost 1000 MT order. The
same agent plans freight: how many containers of which size, whether to stuff at one port, and
whether a small leg goes as LCL. It uses quotes from Cogoport / Freightos / Maersk instead of a
fixed 25 MT per container.

**Order of work.** 13.4 (a deal with several supplier legs) has to exist before 13.3 can approve a
composition with two suppliers. 13.1 and 13.2 can start first behind `DEAL_COMPOSITION_ENABLED`,
with compositions limited to one supplier until 13.4 lands. The decisions on firm-offer wording,
lot mixing and the Shipping Bill come before the first two-supplier trade.

### 14 Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)

Everything a person must do outside the code to run an Indian export-import company legally. The
software cannot issue a PI or PO without most of it (exporter IEC / GSTIN / LUT / AD code on every
document), and escrow and payout providers will not open accounts without the company documents and
a website. Items already listed elsewhere are repeated here with "also n.n" so this area can be used
as the one start-up checklist.

**Order to do it in** (each step needs the one before):
1. Decide the legal form and the registered office (decisions).
2. Directors' DSC → incorporation (CIN, company PAN and TAN).
3. AD Category-I bank current account + EEFC account.
4. GST registration → IEC → ICEGATE + AD code at the ports → LUT for the year.
5. RCMC for each product (Spices Board, APEDA, FIEO) and Udyam; FSSAI / plant quarantine as the products need.
6. Domain, e-mail and website (needed for KYB) → escrow / payout provider accounts → QIMA, Maersk, forwarder, CHA, insurer.
7. CA, compliance calendar and accounting from the first day of trading.

Fees, forms and deadlines change; confirm each step with the CA before filing. The product examples
(spices, basmati) follow the HS codes used in testing; the real list comes from the catalogue.
